          <change when="2018-06-01T14:44:51-04:00" who="https://papyri.info/editor/users/james.cowey">Finalized - Ready.</change>
          <change when="2018-06-01T14:37:46-04:00" who="https://papyri.info/editor/users/james.cowey">Vote - Accept-Straight-to-Finalization - I agree that there is no trace of the omega on the Vienna part of the text. The solution presented here is very appealing.</change>
          <change when="2018-06-01T13:47:32-04:00" who="https://papyri.info/editor/users/dkaltsas">Submit - I do not see any trace of the omega of αὐτῶι on the online image of the Vienna part of the piece; I also think that the space before the iota is too broad for only one letter. Supplementing two letters, [ωτ], we get a phrase making better sense: τί πρὸς σέ; meaning  “what's it to you?” For parallels see Blass – Debrunner – Funk, Grammar NT §127.3 and §239.6. </change>
